.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-status 1  "tapectl-status " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-status \- The home screen: the home and why it was chosen, what is running, the units by state, the volumes due a verify, and the next commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl status\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-last\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
The home screen: the home and why it was chosen, what is running, the units by state, the volumes due a verify, and the next commands
.PP
The home is named with the rule that chose it (see \-\-home). What is running and how the last sessions ended come from the session logs: the live session\*(Aqs phase, progress, rate and ETA, and each recent session\*(Aqs outcome and phase timings. Then, from the catalog, the units by state (pending: never archived or dirty; staged, awaiting a write; under\-copied, below their copy floor), the volumes due a verify (no full readback, or past the verify_interval_days of a unit on them), and at most three next commands: an interrupted write to resume, pending units, staged or under\-copied units, a volume to verify.
.PP
An account in the home\*(Aqs `[ops] group` reads the session logs but not the catalog: it is shown the home and the sessions, and is told that the overview needs the service user (`tapectl\-op status`).
.PP
Exit status: 0 = shown (the catalog read, or an `[ops] group` account shown its part); 2 = the catalog could not be read (not initialized, damaged or busy), said on stderr after the home and the sessions.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format
.TP
\fB\-\-last\fR \fI<LAST>\fR [default: 5]
How many finished sessions to show, newest first (running ones are always shown)
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes. Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable verbose output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr. Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 confirmation prompts. It never reaches a Tier\-3 refusal — those are facts, not risks to accept
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file.

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs.

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
